    "https://ap.washington.edu/ahr/position-details/?job_id=190297": {
      "title": "Position Details",
      "description": "Position Details - Academic Personnel & Faculty Skip to content All the UW Current site Search scope All the UW Current site Enter search text University of Washington University of Washington UW HR Workday Employee Help UW Finance Faculty Senate Menu Home About us About Us Our Strategic Plan Leadership Team Office and Team Contacts Faculty Affairs Advisory Committee News News eDigest Archive For Academic Personnel For Academic Personnel & Faculty New Faculty @UW Faculty Development Faculty Inclusive Excellence ADVANCE Center for Institutional Change Faculty Review Resources Faculty Promotion & Tenure Librarian Reappointment & Promotion Academic Responsibilities Voting Guidelines Time Off and Leaves Postdoctoral Scholars Affairs & Operations Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Academic Titles and Ranks Resources Forms and Templates Policies and Procedures Appointment Actions Visas Academic Jobs Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Academic Personnel & Faculty Affairs Position Details Position Details Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track, Information Systems and Information Management Position Overview School / Campus / College: Information School Organization: Information School Title: Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track, Information Systems and Information Management Position Details Position Description The Information School at the University of Washington invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor, with an anticipated start date of September 1, 2027. We seek applicants who demonstrate commitment to excellence in research, teaching, and service in a diverse and inclusive academic community. The successful candidate will join our faculty on a full-time basis for a nine-month academic appointment, with a 3-year term, renewable beginning 2027. The base salary range for this position will be $13,000 - $14,935 per month, commensurate with experience and qualifications, or as mandated by a U.S. Department of Labor prevailing wage determination. The successful candidate will be expected to: Conduct high-quality research, and pursue extramural funding as needed to support research; Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in their area of specialization; Perform service that furthers the mission of the Information School and the University of Washington more broadly; and Contribute to the Information School’s strategic areas: climate change, health and wellness, healthy information environments, and responsibility in artificial intelligence. This position will contribute to research and teaching in Information Systems and Organizations, with a focus on the organizational integration, deployment, and impact of artificial intelligence (AI). We welcome applicants whose work examines the socio-technical, organizational, strategic, and human dimensions of AI in contemporary organizations. Areas of research interest include, but are not limited to: AI trust, governance, and responsible deployment within organizations AI-enabled transformation of work, organizations, and the future of work Human-AI collaboration, decision-making, and knowledge work AI strategy, digital transformation, and organizational capabilities Organizational adoption, implementation, and management of AI systems Ethical, equitable, and inclusive AI in organizational contexts AI and organizational learning, innovation, and performance All University of Washington faculty are expected to engage in teaching, research, and service. About the University of Washington Information School Information is central to knowledge; to understanding; to impact. Amid the AI transformation, climate change and ongoing threats to public and civic health, information plays a pivotal role in giving everyone the tools to thrive. Our work advances effective information systems and equitable access to information by transforming practices within organizations large and small, and influencing public policy. The UW Information School is leading the global movement to devise information solutions that enrich people’s lives and tackle the world’s toughest challenges through our in-demand undergraduate and graduate level education and our interdisciplinary research centers and partnerships. Together, our students, faculty, alumni and partners are actively creating a future where effective use of information can help everyone discover, learn, innovate, solve problems, and have fun. Information changes lives. Learn more at ischool.uw.edu. Qualifications Applicants for this position must meet the following minimum qualifications: Applicants must minimally hold a PhD (or foreign equivalent) by the date of appointment. Applicants must have 3 years of experience in conducting research, inclusive of graduate studies. Instructions To ensure full consideration of your application, please submit all materials no later than November 1, 2026. Priority will be given to those applications received by October 1, 2026. Applications will only be accepted via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com . The position will remain open until filled. Applications should include the following materials: Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter - Describe briefly your academic and professional background, your areas of research expertise, and your teaching interests. (1 page maximum) Research Statement - Describe your research agenda including your major work to date, your plans for future research, and the sources of potential funding for your research into the future. (3 pages maximum) Teaching Statement - Describe your approach to and experience teaching, in particular at the undergraduate or graduate level. Where appropriate, please include any experience designing courses, instructional materials, or curriculum development. (2 pages maximum) Throughout the materials above, applicants should articulate how their past experiences and/or future plans in research, teaching, mentoring, and/or service promote a sense of belonging and foster a welcoming and inclusive environment. Please also include contact information for 3 referees who are prepared to provide letters of recommendation upon request. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Equal Opportunity The University of Washington is committed to fostering an inclusive, respectful and welcoming community for all. As an equal opportunity employer, the University considers applicants for employment without regard to race, color, creed, religion, national origin, citizenship, sex, pregnancy, age, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information, disability, or veteran status consistent with UW Executive Order No. 81 . Benefits Information The University is committed to providing excellent benefits to our faculty. For a summary of available benefits, please see UWHR benefits information . Privacy Notice Your application materials may be used for employment consideration and shared in accordance with UW’s Privacy Policy. Disability Services The University of Washington is committed to providing access and reasonable accommodation in its services, programs, activities, education, and employment for individuals with disabilities. To request disability accommodation in the application process, contact the Disability Services Office at 206-543-6450 or dso@uw.edu. Apply now The University of Washington is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. For help signing up, accessing your account, or submitting your application, please check out Interfolio's help and support section or get in touch via email at help@interfolio.com or phone at (877)997-8807. Title IX Notice The University of Washington does not discriminate on the basis of sex and prohibits sex discrimination and sex-based harassment in its education programs and activities, including in admission and employment. Refer to the UW policies page on the Title IX website to review (1) the University’s policies prohibiting sex- and gender-based violence, harassment, and discrimination and (2) the related procedures for addressing complaints. Inquiries about Title IX may be referred to the University’s Title IX Coordinator , the U.S. Department of Education’s Office for Civil Rights , or both. To report information or make a complaint about conduct that may constitute sex discrimination or sex-based harassment, submit an online Title IX report or email or call the Office of the Title IX Coordinator. Office of the Title IX Coordinator Valery Richardson, Title IX Coordinator 4311 11th Ave NE Seattle, WA 98105 206-221-7932 TitleIX@uw.edu ← Back to all positions University of Washington Be boundless Connect with us: Facebook Twitter Instagram YouTube LinkedIn Pinterest Accessibility Contact Us Jobs Campus Safety My UW Rules Docket Privacy Terms Newsletter © 2026 University of Washington  |  Seattle, WA"
    },
